WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.443 --> 00:00:07.860
SARAH WELDON: Hello, and welcome
to Engage and Translate--

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:12.040
Text and Audio Chat
in Over 100 Languages.

00:00:12.040 --> 00:00:14.640
I'm Sarah Weldon, Product
Manager for Google Cloud

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:16.650
Translation Services,
which include

00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:19.500
APIs for pre-trained
and customizable machine

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:23.010
translation models, and a
new beta API for supporting

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.320
streaming audio translation.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:26.790
More on that later.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:28.290
I'll be joined
later in this talk

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.148
with Dale Markowitz, Applied
AI engineer at Google Cloud.

00:00:32.148 --> 00:00:33.690
And she'll bring
some of the features

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:38.590
that we are discussing to
life in a couple of demos.

00:00:38.590 --> 00:00:41.650
Today's companies, their
employees, and their customers

00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.840
are truly global.

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:46.720
And every day, they're
also more digital.

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:49.090
Keeping pace with the
exchange of information

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:52.700
in multiple languages at
scale is a growing challenge,

00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:56.110
especially so when more
work is moving to virtual.

00:00:56.110 --> 00:00:58.270
So companies are turning
to machine learning

00:00:58.270 --> 00:01:02.050
to augment their workloads and
to make content more accessible

00:01:02.050 --> 00:01:06.310
faster than would be
possible without automation.

00:01:06.310 --> 00:01:08.560
Luckily, advances in
machine translation

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:10.390
continue to drive up quality.

00:01:10.390 --> 00:01:12.880
And new features on our
products give customers

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.300
more control over how their
content is translated.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:17.930
More on that later.

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:21.910
Let's take a quick look
at where we started.

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:25.810
Pre-2016, the world is
relying on statistical machine

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:26.890
translation.

00:01:26.890 --> 00:01:30.370
As an example for this,
we take a set paragraph

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:35.440
from a Japanese language and
we translated it into English

00:01:35.440 --> 00:01:36.940
for readability.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:39.640
So in this case, this
translates as, Kilimanjaro

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:43.870
is 19,710 feet of the
mountain covered with snow

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:47.230
and it said to be a
highest mountain in Africa.

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.780
It's a little awkward
in how it's written,

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:51.580
as well as there's
some words missing

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.740
if you are a fluent reader.

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:57.880
But if we fast forward to
today, we've done a lot of work

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.850
to improve machine translation
using machine learning.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:06.340
And so Google introduced a
neural machine translation

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:09.160
after 2016 and into 2017.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:12.430
And under that new model,
this same translation

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:14.410
would come through
as, Kilimanjaro

00:02:14.410 --> 00:02:18.700
is a mountain of 19,710
feet covered with snow

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:21.430
and is said to be the
highest mountain in Africa.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:23.200
It's a much more
fluent translation.

00:02:23.200 --> 00:02:24.520
It doesn't feel awkward.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:28.280
And it's a good number of words.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.470
But we keep improving,
even beyond this.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:33.110
In summary, the last major
jump in quality we saw

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.930
was in 2016 to 2017.

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:38.630
But we've continuously improved
over the years as well.

00:02:38.630 --> 00:02:41.750
And in the most recent year,
which you see with this graph

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:46.250
in green, we saw an increased
jump over multiple languages

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:48.470
through a number of different
technology investments

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.290
driven by our research
teams, and an average

00:02:51.290 --> 00:02:56.090
of 5-point blue score jump
over 100-plus languages.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:58.460
And this improvement is
comparable to the gain

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.500
observed years ago
when translating

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:03.290
from statistical to neural
machine translation, as I

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.630
spoke about a few minutes ago.

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:10.650
So our products include
Translation API Basic,

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.795
Translation API Advanced,
and AutoML translation,

00:03:13.795 --> 00:03:15.420
and then the new one
that I was talking

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:17.000
about a little bit later.

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:20.310
For Translation API Basic,
the implementations on it

00:03:20.310 --> 00:03:23.160
are really intended
for simple use cases.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:24.893
It's intended for
user-generated content

00:03:24.893 --> 00:03:26.310
where you're not
trying to control

00:03:26.310 --> 00:03:28.840
a lot of the specific
translations.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:31.980
So we wanted to make
it very simple to use.

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:35.370
And so Translation API Basic
does do important things still

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:36.900
with language detection.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:41.040
And it also continues to use the
advancements of the new machine

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:44.800
learning models as we release
them and develop new languages.

00:03:44.800 --> 00:03:46.620
But you can use
Translation API Basic

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.800
for chat scenarios and
user-generated content,

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.780
because it's easy
to integrate with

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.510
and you're not trying to change
the translations after the fact

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:56.860
for a number of these cases.

00:03:56.860 --> 00:03:59.460
But in this
user-generated content,

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:01.680
it still is really
easy to use and will

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:05.340
enable you to easily
integrate with many languages

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.770
at a very simple platform.

00:04:07.770 --> 00:04:10.050
As an example
case, I'll tell you

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:11.850
a little bit about a
gaming scenario, where

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:15.510
we had one of our customers,
in this case, ELEX,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:19.380
which publishes games
in mobile gaming chat.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.170
And their gamers
are international.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:24.870
And so when the gamers are
wanting to talk to each other

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.900
and collaborate in order
to achieve certain goals,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.150
or to just chat
online, they sometimes

00:04:30.150 --> 00:04:33.000
need to be able to cross
between the multiple languages.

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:37.080
And so by integrating with
the Translation API Basic,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:39.930
ELEX is able to improve
the engagement experience

00:04:39.930 --> 00:04:42.920
for their end users and be able
to improve the overall gaming

00:04:42.920 --> 00:04:47.280
experience just by
connecting in with our APIs.

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:50.160
But this isn't just specific
to gaming or to tech.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.440
It's actually really useful for
government scenarios as well.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:55.410
So let's talk about
another customer scenario,

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.000
this time, public sector.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.190
Within the US, many
languages are spoken,

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:00.690
and governments are
required to make

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.300
information accessible
to their constituents.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:04.860
Let's look at how
machine translation

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.390
can help with this scenario.

00:05:06.390 --> 00:05:09.420
So I'll turn it over to Dale.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.670
DALE MARKOWITZ: Thanks, Sarah.

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:11.210
I'm Dale.

00:05:11.210 --> 00:05:13.520
I'm an applied AI
engineer here at Google.

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.110
And I'm talking to you
from Austin, Texas.

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:17.600
Texas is a really
interesting state

00:05:17.600 --> 00:05:20.150
because it's actually the
second most multilingual state

00:05:20.150 --> 00:05:22.017
in the US, after California.

00:05:22.017 --> 00:05:23.600
And something like
one in three people

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:25.790
don't actually speak English
as their first language

00:05:25.790 --> 00:05:27.060
in their homes.

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.730
So it's really
important, especially in

00:05:28.730 --> 00:05:30.890
times like these,
that the city is

00:05:30.890 --> 00:05:33.050
able to communicate
with constituents

00:05:33.050 --> 00:05:35.100
in their own languages.

00:05:35.100 --> 00:05:37.050
So I recently had heard
about this project.

00:05:37.050 --> 00:05:39.140
It was actually done
by the state of Hawaii,

00:05:39.140 --> 00:05:41.960
where they used the Google
Translate API to automatically

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:43.850
translate a bunch of
legislative documents

00:05:43.850 --> 00:05:45.320
so that all of
their constituents

00:05:45.320 --> 00:05:47.250
could understand the documents.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.360
So I kind of wondered, could I
do the same thing for Austin?

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:53.350
So I decided I would go
to the Austin website,

00:05:53.350 --> 00:05:56.060
and I found a bunch of different
questions that people ask.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.890
Where do I take out
my garbage or how do I

00:05:57.890 --> 00:05:59.900
report a leaking fire hydrant?

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.850
And I decided to
build a chat bot that

00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.630
understands questions and
then gives the city's answers.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:06.470
But I used the
Translation API to be

00:06:06.470 --> 00:06:09.290
able to detect what language
people are asking in,

00:06:09.290 --> 00:06:11.490
and then respond in
their own language.

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.960
So let's take a look.

00:06:13.960 --> 00:06:16.413
This is a chat app that I
built to answer questions

00:06:16.413 --> 00:06:17.455
about the city of Austin.

00:06:20.050 --> 00:06:21.920
It recognizes lots of languages.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:23.770
So for example, if
I ask how to check

00:06:23.770 --> 00:06:26.160
if I'm registered
to vote in Spanish,

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:29.500
it will detect my language
and use the Translation API

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:30.910
to respond in the same language.

00:06:33.550 --> 00:06:36.620
The Translation API supports
over 100 different languages

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:38.310
and lots of different
character sets,

00:06:38.310 --> 00:06:40.143
so you can write your
app code once and have

00:06:40.143 --> 00:06:43.540
it work all over the world.

00:06:43.540 --> 00:06:45.330
So the demo that
I just showed you,

00:06:45.330 --> 00:06:47.970
it was working with a chat bot
that had some preset answers.

00:06:47.970 --> 00:06:50.340
But actually, the architecture
works exactly the same

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:52.060
for any sort of chat--

00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:53.810
like for example, if
I was building a chat

00:06:53.810 --> 00:06:55.260
app to chat with
Sarah and we wanted

00:06:55.260 --> 00:06:56.580
to use two different languages.

00:06:56.580 --> 00:06:58.540
So let me walk you
through how it works.

00:06:58.540 --> 00:06:59.700
So the first thing
you might have noticed

00:06:59.700 --> 00:07:02.242
is that you didn't actually have
to specify what language you

00:07:02.242 --> 00:07:03.300
wanted to use chat in.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:04.843
That's because the
Translation API,

00:07:04.843 --> 00:07:06.510
if you give it enough
text, can actually

00:07:06.510 --> 00:07:08.140
tell what the language is.

00:07:08.140 --> 00:07:10.760
So the text gets sent from
the chat client to a server.

00:07:10.760 --> 00:07:12.813
The Translation API
detects the language.

00:07:12.813 --> 00:07:14.730
And then it translates
the sentence to English

00:07:14.730 --> 00:07:17.460
because my chat bot only
understands English.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.960
Then once the chat
bot replies, we

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:21.330
use the Translation API
again to translate back

00:07:21.330 --> 00:07:24.090
to the sender's language
and send the response back

00:07:24.090 --> 00:07:25.680
to the chat.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:28.470
And that's how you can build
a translation chat app.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:31.860
Now, this worked really well
for the majority of the cases

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:32.638
that I tested.

00:07:32.638 --> 00:07:34.680
But there were some where
it didn't do that well,

00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:36.450
in terms of translation quality.

00:07:36.450 --> 00:07:39.630
And those are the cases where
there was some really specific

00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:43.080
Austin terminology, like
the name of our metro system

00:07:43.080 --> 00:07:47.257
or some really specific
words for things in Austin.

00:07:47.257 --> 00:07:48.840
So I'm going to hand
it back to Sarah,

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:50.798
and she'll tell you how
you can improve quality

00:07:50.798 --> 00:07:54.050
for domain-specific cases.

00:07:54.050 --> 00:07:55.390
SARAH WELDON: Thanks, Dale.

00:07:55.390 --> 00:07:56.032
That was great.

00:07:56.032 --> 00:07:57.490
Now let's talk
about what we can do

00:07:57.490 --> 00:07:59.440
to modify content
to better represent

00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.050
different possible
translations within a language,

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:06.110
depending on the industry
context or location.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:08.980
So in this scenario, I'm
thinking of public sector

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:09.490
again.

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.073
And it's a pretty
common thing to want

00:08:11.073 --> 00:08:15.100
to be able to provide
comments to a public agency--

00:08:15.100 --> 00:08:17.620
in this case, let's say
the attorney general.

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:21.340
Now if you were
talking to users that

00:08:21.340 --> 00:08:24.480
are in Spanish, that
are speaking Spanish,

00:08:24.480 --> 00:08:26.110
there is a pretty
common translation

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:29.810
for attorney general,
which is fiscal general.

00:08:29.810 --> 00:08:32.080
So in this case, our
machine translation

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:36.730
would automatically select this
for the translated response.

00:08:36.730 --> 00:08:39.460
But there are other countries,
say in Latin America,

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.010
that might have a different
version of what they call

00:08:42.010 --> 00:08:44.500
their attorney general's
office-- in this case,

00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:47.780
Ministerio Publico Fiscal.

00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:50.890
But even in California,
they actually

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:52.462
have a pre-described glossary.

00:08:52.462 --> 00:08:53.920
And if you look at
that, they refer

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:55.795
to their attorney
general's office in Spanish

00:08:55.795 --> 00:08:58.190
as Procurador General.

00:08:58.190 --> 00:09:00.490
So what if you could
customize your translations

00:09:00.490 --> 00:09:02.650
to your domain?

00:09:02.650 --> 00:09:06.810
With Translation API Advanced,
you can actually do this.

00:09:06.810 --> 00:09:09.500
And if you have a
terminology base, which

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:12.770
a lot of public organizations
do, and companies do,

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:17.150
as well, then you can use
this as your starting point

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.270
with an easy integration with
Translation API Advanced.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:23.060
Because you have your
English terms and your terms

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.280
in other target
languages, if you just

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:29.480
create this into a CSV file
and import it into your Google

00:09:29.480 --> 00:09:32.450
Cloud project, then
you can use this

00:09:32.450 --> 00:09:34.820
with future cases for
machine translation

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:37.910
just by referencing your
glossary in your Translation

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:40.610
API call, and then when
you get the response back,

00:09:40.610 --> 00:09:43.490
it'll actually preserve
your preferred term

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:45.590
over what the machine
translation might

00:09:45.590 --> 00:09:47.690
have otherwise predicted.

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:50.690
And Translation
API Advanced also

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.600
has other features that
can help with both scaling

00:09:53.600 --> 00:09:56.930
your implementation, as well
as more advanced customization

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:01.040
capabilities with our custom
machine translation models.

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:04.310
All of that can be done
through one API integrated

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:07.310
with your GCP project.

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:10.090
But let's say you have even
more than just terminology

00:10:10.090 --> 00:10:11.310
and you want to go further.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:13.510
You're company or
organization that

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.300
has a lot of past
human translations.

00:10:16.300 --> 00:10:19.240
And you want to be able to use
those translations to improve

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:21.880
the machine translation
prediction for what

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:25.630
a translation would likely be
for your content, your context,

00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:28.070
or your industry.

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:31.630
So in that case,
it's really important

00:10:31.630 --> 00:10:33.370
to understand
conceptually that Google

00:10:33.370 --> 00:10:36.160
has both pre-trained models,
which is what you typically

00:10:36.160 --> 00:10:40.900
use with machine Translation
API Advanced or Translation API

00:10:40.900 --> 00:10:42.105
Basic.

00:10:42.105 --> 00:10:44.230
And these are pre-trained
models that we've created

00:10:44.230 --> 00:10:45.575
using a lot of training data.

00:10:45.575 --> 00:10:47.200
But if you have your
own training data,

00:10:47.200 --> 00:10:49.750
you can actually use
those as a starting point,

00:10:49.750 --> 00:10:51.370
and then build and
improve on them

00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:55.030
with a custom training
on AutoML Translation.

00:10:55.030 --> 00:10:58.240
So by taking your past
human translations,

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:01.660
bringing that to our platform,
and running trainings on it,

00:11:01.660 --> 00:11:05.680
then you're improving the
statistical and mathematical

00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:07.930
likelihood that our model
is going to provide back

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:11.620
to you a translation that's
tuned to your context

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:14.420
and to your industry.

00:11:14.420 --> 00:11:16.690
So it does this through
transfer learning.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.160
And it has an entire
platform where

00:11:18.160 --> 00:11:21.310
you can go and learn and
manage more of these models

00:11:21.310 --> 00:11:25.480
to integrate it with more
advancements in your platform.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.940
But now, let's go back
to our chat scenario.

00:11:27.940 --> 00:11:33.620
Our chat app uses the right
language, but it doesn't speak.

00:11:33.620 --> 00:11:35.940
It's actually just
using text translation.

00:11:35.940 --> 00:11:39.530
So since users both
write and talk,

00:11:39.530 --> 00:11:42.050
there are scenarios where
we might want to have audio,

00:11:42.050 --> 00:11:47.450
as well as text translation
supported for our use cases.

00:11:47.450 --> 00:11:51.430
So can we translate
speech in real time?

00:11:51.430 --> 00:11:54.160
Organizations are seeing value
in delivering their products

00:11:54.160 --> 00:11:55.670
and content globally.

00:11:55.670 --> 00:11:56.980
And it's not just in text.

00:11:56.980 --> 00:11:57.940
It's in video.

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.080
It's in audio.

00:11:59.080 --> 00:12:00.610
It's in calls.

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:02.800
And so for these
scenarios, you want

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:06.520
to be able to have an easier
way to implement translations

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:07.980
for those use cases.

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:09.910
And it's applicable in
media entertainment.

00:12:09.910 --> 00:12:11.422
It's applicable in technology.

00:12:11.422 --> 00:12:13.130
And it's applicable
for service providers

00:12:13.130 --> 00:12:16.780
that might be trying to
support customer support cases.

00:12:16.780 --> 00:12:19.540
But what's so complicated
about translating nontext data

00:12:19.540 --> 00:12:20.770
with audio and video?

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:23.260
We have our speech
APIs and our video APIs

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:26.320
that allow for interacting
with those types of content.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.120
And we have translations,
so we can just

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:30.100
integrate between
all three of them

00:12:30.100 --> 00:12:32.670
in order to be able
to get an outcome.

00:12:32.670 --> 00:12:34.143
But it's kind of hard to do.

00:12:34.143 --> 00:12:35.560
And it comes with
other challenges

00:12:35.560 --> 00:12:37.690
that are nearly
impossible to solve

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:39.380
on the developer-client side.

00:12:39.380 --> 00:12:42.760
So we're looking at how to
solve that on the cloud side.

00:12:42.760 --> 00:12:46.060
For one thing, latency
can be a lot higher

00:12:46.060 --> 00:12:49.340
if you're integrating across
multiple APIs, especially

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:51.340
if one is set up for
streaming and the other one

00:12:51.340 --> 00:12:53.090
is setup for more
synchronous cases, where

00:12:53.090 --> 00:12:55.930
it's waiting for the
content to finish

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:59.620
before it starts to figuring
out what a translation would be.

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:02.930
Additionally, you have
to do multiple API calls.

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:05.110
So that can be a
complexity for developers

00:13:05.110 --> 00:13:07.443
to work with, especially if
they're trying to figure out

00:13:07.443 --> 00:13:09.010
how to improve
accuracy, et cetera,

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:11.350
across these two different APIs.

00:13:11.350 --> 00:13:13.960
And then you're also dealing
with a streaming client,

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:16.480
which creates some
complexities with integrations

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:17.380
and usability.

00:13:17.380 --> 00:13:21.640
And it's a challenge in itself.

00:13:21.640 --> 00:13:25.100
So Media Translation API
simplifies this process.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:29.160
It supports directly audio
files or microphone input,

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:31.850
say through a mobile phone
or through your browser.

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:35.180
And it also accepts
that content directly,

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:37.958
so you're not having to run
through a speech model first

00:13:37.958 --> 00:13:39.500
and then a translation
model in order

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:41.480
to get a translation back.

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:44.060
You can go directly
to Media Translation

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:46.340
API with your audio,
and you'll get back

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:48.890
a text translation
in the language which

00:13:48.890 --> 00:13:51.140
you've set as your target
language for translation

00:13:51.140 --> 00:13:53.840
outcome.

00:13:53.840 --> 00:13:56.920
In addition to abstracting away
the frictions of multiple API

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.070
calls, we have some
other features that

00:14:00.070 --> 00:14:01.870
overall improve the experience.

00:14:01.870 --> 00:14:04.240
So automatic
punctuation detection,

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.780
as well as using
enhanced models for video

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.700
or telephoning use
cases allows you

00:14:09.700 --> 00:14:14.740
to be able to make the most to
get the best quality optimized

00:14:14.740 --> 00:14:18.665
for your audio
translation outputs.

00:14:18.665 --> 00:14:20.290
And then we also have
language support,

00:14:20.290 --> 00:14:21.940
because it's a
translation model.

00:14:21.940 --> 00:14:25.210
So we support French, German,
Italian, Hindi, Portuguese,

00:14:25.210 --> 00:14:28.300
Russian, Spanish,
English, and others,

00:14:28.300 --> 00:14:30.550
for you to be able to
translate multiple languages

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:32.510
for your user base.

00:14:32.510 --> 00:14:35.090
So with that, I'll
take it back to Dale

00:14:35.090 --> 00:14:39.645
so we can see the Media
Translation API in action.

00:14:39.645 --> 00:14:41.020
DALE MARKOWITZ:
Thank you, Sarah.

00:14:41.020 --> 00:14:42.810
So as a developer, I
can confirm that it

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:45.180
is tricky to do anything
in real time, especially

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:46.520
real-time translation.

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.870
But the Media Translation
API makes this a lot easier,

00:14:48.870 --> 00:14:51.120
which is great, because there
are so many times I want

00:14:51.120 --> 00:14:52.740
to do real-time translation.

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:54.840
Like imagine if I'm
doing a live stream

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:57.540
and I want users all around
the world to understand it.

00:14:57.540 --> 00:14:59.280
Or maybe I'm giving
a talk in person

00:14:59.280 --> 00:15:00.900
and I want different
audience members

00:15:00.900 --> 00:15:03.330
with different languages
to get what I'm saying.

00:15:03.330 --> 00:15:04.747
So I'm going to
show you something

00:15:04.747 --> 00:15:06.580
that I built with the
Media Translation API

00:15:06.580 --> 00:15:09.220
and the Raspberry Pi
and the Mini Projector.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:12.140
And let's take a look.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.470
Hi, my name is Dale.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:20.960
I'm using the Media Translation
API and a Raspberry Pi

00:15:20.960 --> 00:15:22.955
to translate what I'm
saying in real time.

00:15:25.650 --> 00:15:27.870
So that is what I'm bringing
to my talk next time

00:15:27.870 --> 00:15:30.245
I give a talk somewhere that
speaks a different language.

00:15:30.245 --> 00:15:32.210
And with that, I'll
give it back to Sarah.

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:33.890
SARAH WELDON: Thanks, Dale.

00:15:33.890 --> 00:15:36.800
I wanted to close with
some thoughts from one

00:15:36.800 --> 00:15:40.850
of our translation customers
for Media Translation API.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:43.550
When they integrated with
our Media Translation API,

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:46.190
they were able to see
games in low latency,

00:15:46.190 --> 00:15:49.010
as well as the overall
quality of performance.

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:51.230
And so they're able to
provide real-time streaming

00:15:51.230 --> 00:15:53.930
translation for video chat.

00:15:53.930 --> 00:15:56.210
OnePlus is a phone provider.

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:58.430
And they've created
a beta application

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:01.820
that allows for their customers
to be able to opt in and be

00:16:01.820 --> 00:16:03.725
able to see the
translations from the person

00:16:03.725 --> 00:16:05.600
that they're speaking
in a different language

00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:09.060
while they're still engaged
with them in video chat.

00:16:09.060 --> 00:16:11.390
And so with this, we're
able to demonstrate

00:16:11.390 --> 00:16:14.030
how text translation
and audio translation

00:16:14.030 --> 00:16:17.090
can work seamlessly together
to create a better end

00:16:17.090 --> 00:16:20.020
user experience.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:23.290
To close, I wanted to point
you to our documentation

00:16:23.290 --> 00:16:26.260
on our Cloud website, where
you can get more information

00:16:26.260 --> 00:16:28.990
on AutoML translation,
Translation API

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.780
Advanced, and our new
Media Translation API.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:32.860
Please check them out.

00:16:32.860 --> 00:16:35.010
Thanks for joining us.

